<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  #102 resource sizing: assert server-wide memory behavior instead of riding
  stock-image defaults. CH 24.8 reads the cgroup-v2 limit; the ratio below
  bounds the server at CH_MEM_RATIO x cgroup memory (vendor-recommended 0.9 —
  headroom for allocator overhead and untracked native memory). The values
  come from the resource plan (scripts/resource_planner.py) via the compose
  environment; compose supplies defaults equal to today's behavior, so an
  unset plan changes nothing.

  ==========================================================================
  P2 MERGE-MEMORY BUDGET (docs/scale/P2_CLICKHOUSE_MEMFLAT_2026-08-29.md,
  run p2-s04b-08290858). MEASURED, on the 2.5K leg:

    * cgroup limit (CLICKHOUSE_MEM_LIMIT=5326m) ...... 5,584,715,776 B = 5,326 MiB
      (CH agrees: asynchronous_metrics CGroupMemoryTotal = 5,584,715,776)
    * max_server_memory_usage = 0.9 x cgroup ......... 5,026,244,198 B = 4,794 MiB
    * peak CurrentMetric_MemoryTracking .............. 4,566 MiB = 95.2 % of that cap
    * peak MergesMutationsMemoryTracking ............. 3,978 MiB (merges ALONE)
    * merge write amplification ...................... ~241x (1.40 GiB inserted
      -> 337.6 GiB merged in 75 min, 69,201 merges)

  The server was running at 95 % of its own ceiling with 83 % of it held by
  background merges. Nothing here changes what the engine writes; it bounds
  what ClickHouse is allowed to spend re-merging it. The per-table half of the
  fix (max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool, min_age_to_force_merge_seconds)
  lives in init.sql + chschema/corr_merge_budget.go.

  RESOLUTION SEMANTICS (CH 24.8, Server.cpp): for BOTH max_server_memory_usage
  and merges_mutations_memory_usage_soft_limit, 0 means "derive from the ratio"
  and a non-zero value is LOWERED to the ratio-derived value if it exceeds it —
  i.e. the effective limit is min(explicit, ratio x cgroup). So the explicit
  bytes below are a measured ABSOLUTE cap and the ratios stay live as the
  relative safety net on a smaller box. Setting CH_MAX_SERVER_MEMORY=0 /
  CH_MERGES_MEM=0 restores the pre-P2 ratio-only behavior exactly.
  ==========================================================================
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!-- ARITHMETIC (see the measured block above):
           cgroup limit ............................ 5,326 MiB
           - page-cache / slab / untracked headroom  1,230 MiB (23.1 %)
           = max_server_memory_usage ............... 4,096 MiB = 4,294,967,296 B

         Why 1,230 MiB of headroom and not the 532 MiB the 0.9 ratio left:
         the cgroup holds reclaimable kernel memory the server does not
         account for — measured at the s04b peak, active_file 1,516 MiB +
         slab_reclaimable 621 MiB = 2,137 MiB, against anon 984 MiB. Held at
         0.9 the server may reserve 4,794 MiB and leave the kernel 532 MiB to
         cache a store that reads/writes ~50 GiB per run. Reserving >= 1 GiB
         and rounding DOWN to the 4,096 MiB power of two gives 1,230 MiB.
         MemoryTracking and RSS also couple loosely (peak 4,566 MiB tracked
         vs 984 MiB anon), so the cap is a reservation ceiling, not an RSS
         prediction — which is exactly why the memflat gate must read anon,
         not `docker stats` (brief section (c)).

         Cross-check against the proposed memflat clause: the OOM assertion is
         peak MemoryTracking < 85 % of this cap = 3,482 MiB (s04b measured
         4,566 -> would FAIL, which is the finding worth gating on). -->
    <max_server_memory_usage from_env="CH_MAX_SERVER_MEMORY"/>
    <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio from_env="CH_MEM_RATIO"/>

    <!-- MEASURED: merges alone peaked at 3,978 MiB. Left at 0 the soft limit
         is derived as merges_mutations_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio (0.5) x
         cgroup = 2,792,357,888 B = 2,663 MiB — which the run BLEW THROUGH by
         49 %, because the limit is checked when a merge is SCHEDULED, not
         while it runs: 32 already-running merges (background_pool_size 16 x
         concurrency ratio 2) overshoot it together. So this value is one half
         of the fix and background_pool_size below is the other.
         1.5 GiB = 1,610,612,736 B = 37.5 % of the 4,096 MiB server cap, under
         the memflat clause's proposed "merges < 50 % of the cap" assertion
         (s04b: 3,978 / 4,794 = 83 % -> FAIL). -->
    <merges_mutations_memory_usage_soft_limit from_env="CH_MERGES_MEM"/>

    <!-- MEASURED: 32 concurrent merge tasks (16 x 2) on a box limited to
         3 CPUs (CLICKHOUSE_CPU_LIMIT, host has 4 cores) is what let
         MergesMutationsMemoryTracking reach 3,978 MiB — each concurrent merge
         holds its own read/write buffers for a 26.9 KiB/row table. 6 x 2 = 12
         concurrent merges: still 4x the CPU allowance, so merges are never
         starved, but the memory a merge storm can hold is cut ~2.7x.
         The concurrency ratio is pinned at its default so "12" is a contract
         and not an inherited number.
         NOTE: system.server_settings reports background_pool_size as
         changeable_without_restart = IncreaseOnly — a DECREASE (16 -> 6) needs
         a server restart, i.e. the next stack redeploy. -->
    <background_pool_size>6</background_pool_size>
    <background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio>2</background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio>

    <!-- ===================================================================
         SHRINKING THE POOL IS NOT A ONE-LINE CHANGE. READ THIS FIRST.

         WHICH CHECK FIRES: MergeTreeSettings::sanityCheck(background_pool_tasks)
         in src/Storages/MergeTree/MergeTreeSettings.cpp, called as every
         MergeTree table is ATTACHED at startup. background_pool_tasks =
         background_pool_size x background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio
         = 6 x 2 = 12. It throws BAD_ARGUMENTS — the server never finishes
         loading and crash-loops. THIS HAPPENED: 2026-08-29, exit 36, on the
         first deploy of the P2 budget, because the pool went 16 -> 6 while the
         thresholds below kept their stock values.

         In v24.8 sanityCheck guards EXACTLY THREE of these (verified against
         the 24.8 source; there is no fourth in MergeTreeSettings.h), each as
         `if (threshold > background_pool_tasks) throw`:

           setting                                    stock  here
           number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_lower_max_size_of_merge      8    4
           number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation            20    6
           number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_optimize_entire_partition
                                                                         25    8

         The stock values were sized for the default 16 x 2 = 32 pool, so 20 and
         25 both exceed a 12-slot pool. (8 did not — but it is scaled here too,
         because leaving it alone would silently change its MEANING relative to
         the pool.) The operator is `>`, so a threshold exactly equal to 12
         would be legal; the contract test asserts STRICTLY below 12 anyway, to
         keep a slot of margin and to stay correct if a future version tightens
         the comparison.

         SEMANTICS: each is a floor on FREE pool entries — "when fewer than N
         entries are free, do NOT do X". So a HIGHER number is MORE conservative
         (X is refused sooner as the pool fills), and the three must stay in
         ascending order: shrink merge sizes first, then stop mutations, then
         stop whole-partition optimizes.

         WHY 4 / 6 / 8 on a 12-slot pool (stock proportions on 32 would scale to
         3 / 7.5 / 9.4):

         * lower_max_size_of_merge = 4 (33 % free) — one notch MORE conservative
           than stock (25 % free): merges start shrinking once the pool is 2/3
           busy. That is the direction this whole change exists to push, and it
           is the second line of defence behind
           max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool.
         * execute_mutation = 6 (50 % free) — one notch MORE permissive than
           stock (62.5 %). Mutations are RARE on this platform by design: the
           retention contract is metadata-only (materialize_ttl_after_modify=0)
           and expiry is part drops (ttl_only_drop_parts=1), so essentially the
           only mutation is an operator-initiated one — e.g. the documented
           `MATERIALIZE INDEX idx_archived_for` on corr_signals_archive. Those
           must not be starved by a pool this small.
         * execute_optimize_entire_partition = 8 (67 % free) — LOAD-BEARING for
           the per-table half of this change, not incidental. Its own 24.8
           description says the merge it gates "is created when set
           min_age_to_force_merge_seconds > 0 and
           min_age_to_force_merge_on_partition_only = true" — which is exactly
           what init.sql now sets on all eight corr_* tables. Scaled strictly
           from stock this would be 9; 8 is deliberately one notch lower so the
           idle-consolidation pass can actually get a slot once a burst drains.
           Set it too high and the small parts the merge cap leaves behind never
           get folded at all.

         Pinned by tests/test_clickhouse_merge_budget.py, which recomputes
         pool x ratio from THIS file and re-checks every threshold — including
         the ones left at their ClickHouse default. That test is the one that
         would have caught the crash-loop.
         =================================================================== -->
    <merge_tree>
        <number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_lower_max_size_of_merge>4</number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_lower_max_size_of_merge>
        <number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation>6</number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation>
        <number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_optimize_entire_partition>8</number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_optimize_entire_partition>
    </merge_tree>

    <!-- MEASURED: the stock mark cache ceiling is 5,368,709,120 B = 5,120 MiB
         — larger than the ENTIRE 5,326 MiB container and 107 % of the old
         server cap. Actual use during the run: MarkCacheBytes 2.01 MiB. A
         ceiling that can never be honoured is not a cache policy, it is a
         latent OOM. 512 MiB = 536,870,912 B is 250x the measured working set
         and 12.5 % of the server cap. -->
    <mark_cache_size>536870912</mark_cache_size>

    <!-- MEASURED: UncompressedCacheBytes 0 B — the cache is OFF
         (use_uncompressed_cache = 0, the ClickHouse default, and nothing here
         turns it on), yet the image reserves 8,589,934,592 B = 8,192 MiB for
         it. Zero the reservation so it cannot become live by accident.
         Together with the mark cache this cuts the reservable cache ceiling
         from 13,312 MiB to 512 MiB. -->
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>

    <max_concurrent_queries from_env="CH_MAX_CONCURRENT_QUERIES"/>
</clickhouse>
